WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.540
Now we define a signal.

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:05.157
The DBC BUILDER is where a guess becomes a definition.

00:00:05.157 --> 00:00:09.176
We are describing the front-left wheel speed on message 0A6:

00:00:09.176 --> 00:00:12.391
sixteen bits, big-endian, starting at bit seven,

00:00:12.391 --> 00:00:15.873
scaled by one thirty-second of a kilometre per hour.

00:00:15.873 --> 00:00:21.433
The bit grid underneath shows the live payload with the
selected field highlighted,

00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:25.652
and it converts between the grid and DBC bit numbering for
you,

00:00:25.652 --> 00:00:28.800
which is the part everybody gets wrong by hand.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.854
Underneath the editor is the live decode preview:

00:00:31.854 --> 00:00:37.774
real frames from the capture, their raw bytes, and the
physical value your definition produces.

00:00:37.774 --> 00:00:43.881
Sixty to eighty kilometres per hour here, which is the right
order of magnitude for a wheel speed,

00:00:43.881 --> 00:00:45.501
so the scale is plausible.

00:00:45.501 --> 00:00:51.608
Every decode in CanLab runs through cantools, the same library
that will read the file you export,

00:00:51.608 --> 00:00:54.288
so what you see here is what you get later.

00:00:54.288 --> 00:00:56.270
The PLOT tab draws it over time.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:00.977
Raw bytes and decoded signals can be plotted together on a
shared time axis,

00:01:00.977 --> 00:01:06.613
each with its own scale, so you can compare a decoded value
against the bytes it came from,

00:01:06.613 --> 00:01:09.091
or against a different message entirely.

00:01:09.091 --> 00:01:14.231
A wheel speed that rises and falls smoothly is a good sign the
definition is right;

00:01:14.231 --> 00:01:17.328
a sawtooth would tell you the byte order is wrong.

00:01:17.328 --> 00:01:21.334
INTELLIGENCE looks across messages rather than within one.

00:01:21.334 --> 00:01:27.758
It measures how often each ID repeats, so you can tell a
hundred-hertz control message from a

00:01:27.758 --> 00:01:29.415
one-hertz status report.

00:01:29.415 --> 00:01:33.491
It correlates bytes between different IDs with a lag sweep,

00:01:33.491 --> 00:01:38.325
which is how you find the same physical quantity reported by
two ECUs.

00:01:38.325 --> 00:01:43.091
It diffs two captures to show what changed when you pressed a
button.

00:01:43.091 --> 00:01:49.584
And it decodes parameter group numbers, both J1939 for heavy
vehicles and NMEA 2000 for boats.

00:01:49.584 --> 00:01:51.559
ML INTEL goes further per byte.

00:01:51.559 --> 00:01:57.548
It classifies each byte as a counter, a checksum, a boolean
flag, a physical value or padding,

00:01:57.548 --> 00:02:00.351
with a confidence and the entropy behind it.

00:02:00.351 --> 00:02:05.193
It fits a baseline from normal traffic and then scores frames
for anomalies,

00:02:05.193 --> 00:02:10.736
which is how you find the one message that behaves differently
when a fault is present.

00:02:10.736 --> 00:02:16.152
It also detects change points and finds messages with similar
behaviour by embedding.

00:02:16.152 --> 00:02:17.968
The DASHBOARD is the overview.

00:02:17.968 --> 00:02:22.691
The heatmap shows how much each byte of each message moves
across the capture,

00:02:22.691 --> 00:02:27.414
so a dense column is a message worth investigating and a blank
one is padding.

00:02:27.414 --> 00:02:30.986
There is a message timeline showing when each ID is active,

00:02:30.986 --> 00:02:34.377
and gauges you can point at any signal you have defined,

00:02:34.377 --> 00:02:38.736
so a decoded value can be watched as an instrument rather than
a number.
